yer’s cue ball and the red ball on the same shot; and it is worth two points."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(baseball, figuratively, informal) The arm of a player who can throw well.", "tags": ["countable", "figuratively", "informal", "uncountable"], "examples": ["He’s got a cannon out in right."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(engineering) A hollow cylindrical piece carried by a revolving shaft, on which it may, however, revolve independently.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(historical) A cylindrical item of plate armor protecting the arm, particularly one of a pair of such cylinders worn with a couter, the upper cannon protecting the upper arm and the lower cannon protecting the forearm.", "tags": ["countable", "historical", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The pauldrons are rather weak, but the cannons of the vambraces are good and come from an Italian armour of considerably earlier date, for they have the tulip form of the first half of the century.", "reference": "1949, The Connoisseur:"}, {"text": "During the second half of the century the upper cannons were often joined to the pauldrons […] Here the cannons and the couter, although separate, are joined together when worn by the points securing them to the arming […]", "reference": "1972, Claude Blair, European Armour: Circa 1066 to Circa 1700:"}, {"text": "The breastplate was now usually globular in shape with attached tassets. The arm defenses were almost always in one piece, the lower and upper cannons joined permanently to the couter with internal leathers and rivets, and the whole frequently also joined permanently to the pauldron.", "reference": "2007, Kelly DeVries, Robert Douglas Smith, Medieval Weapons: An Illustrated History of Their Impact, ABC-CLIO, →ISBN, page 178:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(printing, uncountable) Alternative form of canon (“a large size of type”).", "tags": ["alt of", "alternative", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(xiangqi) A piece which moves horizontally and vertically like a rook but captures another piece by jumping over a different piece in the line of attack.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(US, slang) A pickpocket.", "tags": ["US", "countable", "slang", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I also learned never to conspicuoulsy^([sic]) watch a cannon while he was working. Pickpockets dislike being watched, even by those who may be \"right,\" because they become uneasy and clumsy and feel conspicuous.", "reference": "1977, Robert S. Weppner, Street ethnography, page 70:"}, {"text": "A good pickpocket is known to his fellows as a pistol. Rufus Dayne is a cannon. One of the best pickpockets in the country, he makes close to a million dollars a year and has no criminal record at all.", "reference": "2009, James Thomson, Bedlam City: Savage Worlds Edition, page 377:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈkæn.ən/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "cannons", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "cannoning", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "cannoned", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "cannoned", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To bombard with cannons.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonym